WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:03.430
Film Courage: What makes a great storyteller?

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:05.529
Writer/Instructor and Script Consultant Shannan
E. Johnson (The Professional Pen): I think

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:10.840
what makes a great storyteller is someone
who is open to the collaborative process.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:15.349
Open to knowing that as we’re taught when
we’re little two heads are better than one.

00:00:15.349 --> 00:00:19.840
But for whatever reason we become adults and
we want to do everything by ourselves.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.840
So I think great storytellers understand their
part of the process. They understand that

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:29.609
other people can bring stuff to the process.
They are open to utilizing what is happening

00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:31.609
in the world around them.

00:00:31.609 --> 00:00:36.790
For example I traveled for a decade as a touring
artist. I was always in different places so

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:41.290
that means I met different kinds of people,
I saw different kinds of places and we never

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:47.370
really pay attention to the fact that because
I was in that area or around those people

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:52.020
I probably picked up on some new information.
So we are always told to write what we know

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.940
and that automatically becomes so narrow to
us when we think about how can that be what

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:57.940
I know?

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.660
But it’s like if you consider the places
that you’ve been, the people that you’ve

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:04.809
met, what your spouse may do for a living,
what your parents may have done for a living,

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:09.470
what your friends may be doing, what they
may be going through in your personal lives.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:14.170
If you were a child and you watched how mom
and dad X, Y &amp; Z, all of these things are

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:18.939
things that you know and therefore things
that you can write about. So you just have

00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:26.439
to be open. Storytellers have to be open to
notes. I think we all know as artists you

00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:32.249
have to be to criticism but criticism is different
from notes. A lot of the times writers aren’t

00:01:32.249 --> 00:01:37.350
able to tell the difference. They are thinking
the executives are coming in here to steal

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:44.609
my joy and to rain on my parade and really
they are coming in with a totally different

00:01:44.609 --> 00:01:50.430
part of the process. They are coming in from
a totally different spectrum to look at your

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:54.419
script and nine times out of ten if they have
a note, all that means is that there is a

00:01:54.419 --> 00:01:55.880
problem here.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:00.619
You can decide to fix it in any creative way
sometimes they will tell you no this is the

00:02:00.619 --> 00:02:04.759
way it has to be and that is usually because
again they are coming from a different place.

00:02:04.759 --> 00:02:07.499
They may know something about the location
that you don’t know. They may know something

00:02:07.499 --> 00:02:10.209
about permits that you don’t know. They
may know something about the actors that you

00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:16.260
don’t know. They may have information that
again if they are pushing towards their brand

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.629
and whatever it is that you are writing now
seems to be taking them out of that brand

00:02:19.629 --> 00:02:23.141
then they are going to be saying nope alright
let’s take this out, let’s do this or

00:02:23.141 --> 00:02:24.141
do that.

00:02:24.141 --> 00:02:27.830
But I think as creatives we will take that
personally and think that’s criticism instead

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:31.150
of understanding they are doing their part
on the team, you are doing your part on the

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:37.209
team so don’t take it personally or negatively
just realize that they are coming into this

00:02:37.209 --> 00:02:40.780
situation from a different perspective so
storytellers have to be open to that too.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:43.700
So as much as we have to respect the art,
we also have to understand that there is a

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:48.549
business side of the art and a lot of times
that means you may have to compromise your

00:02:48.549 --> 00:02:55.840
art to the people who are paying for it which
again goes back to that whole baby thing right?

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:01.040
Like the point of you writing is so that other
people can see it unless you have your own

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:06.200
money because if someone else buys it. And
once they buy it it is now theirs. So they

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:11.000
are the ones who get to say what direction
to go into and what schools it’s going to

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.280
go to and all of these other things right
where you consider your child.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:20.079
So just being open to understanding as a storyteller
there is an art side and there is a business

00:03:20.079 --> 00:03:21.900
side I think that is really big.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:28.150
Film Courage: Have you seen someone who has
such amazing talent as a writer but the notes

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:34.769
process ruins their career because they were
never able to be collaborative?

00:03:34.769 --> 00:03:44.900
Shannan: Yes. But I think it probably happens
more often than we really know. Being on a

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:51.909
writing staff means that A) you are on the
staff right now but B) you have to see if

00:03:51.909 --> 00:03:55.889
the show is going to get picked back up. If
it does it doesn’t mean that you are automatically

00:03:55.889 --> 00:04:00.260
picked back up. And one of the ways that you
can automatically NOT get picked back up is

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:03.099
that you don’t take notes well.

00:04:03.099 --> 00:04:07.829
So some people are often not getting picked
back up on the next season simply because

00:04:07.829 --> 00:04:11.870
they couldn’t take notes well and again
(especially if you’re on a staff) you’re

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:13.939
here to write someone else’s baby.

00:04:13.939 --> 00:04:22.759
So your opinion matters BUT you writing what
you’re being asked to write matters more.

00:04:22.759 --> 00:04:26.729
There are people who just need, it’s like
a learning curve for them. I can’t necessarily

00:04:26.729 --> 00:04:30.460
say that they never work again. Maybe they
learn from that and they were able to work

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:31.600
again.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:39.460
There also may have been times when people
in authority are kind of mismanaging how they

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:46.700
are doing the writing but attaching themselves
to someone else’s writing in that kind of

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:52.490
room process without realizing that the writer
is doing the writing. Just because you’re

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:56.680
giving notes on the writing doesn’t make
you a writer.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:00.780
And so a lot of the times depending on where
their producers or show runners or directors

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.789
or whatever it is that they are they may be
getting confused in that part of the process

00:05:04.789 --> 00:05:08.099
and now they attach themselves to the writing
and I know there have been people who have

00:05:08.099 --> 00:05:13.930
lost their jobs because of things like that
as well simply because that is what their

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:18.780
job is and this is what your job is. Just
because they work together doesn’t mean

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:24.879
that they are now teams or co-writers or co-whatever.
So that can happen as well but that again

00:05:24.879 --> 00:05:29.030
goes back to people not really understanding
how that part of the industry works and some

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:32.389
people have to learn by trial and error and
it happens that way.

00:05:32.389 --> 00:05:37.990
Film Courage: Okay so I never would have thought
of that. So it’s not just the writer possibly

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:43.520
getting hung up on these notes, it’s also
the note giver not realizing their role as

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:44.520
well?

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.180
Shannon: Their role as well. It’s like you’re
the note giver, that’s part of your responsibility,

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:51.891
that doesn’t make you writer because note
givers may also give suggestions right? And

00:05:51.891 --> 00:05:56.870
so now they are thinking well I gave X, Y
&amp; Z suggestions so now it’s you and I who

00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:02.330
have written this thing. It’s like no, writers
are the ones who are physically writing, right?

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:07.310
If you are a producer, a showrunner, or a
director when it comes to features right you

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:12.270
may be adding possibly but if you’re not
doing…and the WGA [Writers Guild of America]

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:16.720
has rules about if you didn’t do a particular
percentage (I’m not going to try and figure

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:20.289
out and remember what the percentage was)
but if you didn’t do a percent of the physical

00:06:20.289 --> 00:06:22.819
writing, you cannot put your name on it as
a writer.

00:06:22.819 --> 00:06:25.820
Now in features things are going to be a little
different anyway because the writer is taken

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:31.870
out so early in the process. So there could
be other people who have come in and actually

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:36.650
done more writing and there is still a percentage
of writing that they have to do before their

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:40.400
name gets attached but there is a different
in how their name gets attached. It might

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:48.051
say if you see the word A-N-D then that means
it was an extra person who got attached. If

00:06:48.051 --> 00:06:52.530
you see the ampersand sign that means they
are a team. That means they wrote it together.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:58.490
Me and this person wrote this script together.
If you see the word “and” it means they

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:02.840
wrote it and then somebody else came in and
put their part and somebody else came in and

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:08.860
put their part. So you might see one person’s
name and 50 other “ands.” Because all

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:12.050
of these other people came in and they put
in enough of a percentage for them to be getting

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:13.080
credit.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:17.729
Some people who are rewriters are simply ghost
writers and they never get a credit but they

00:07:17.729 --> 00:07:20.409
might have come in and done some writing.

00:07:20.409 --> 00:07:24.340
But then once that writing process is over
and now that script is in the hand of the

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:30.170
director, he or she is going to go in and
change up all kinds of stuff. It doesn’t

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:35.460
make him or her the writer. So you are rarely
going to see suddenly just because now that

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:39.060
the development is over and it’s in production
and the director is now doing things with

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:44.050
the script, it’s not going to necessarily
throw his or her name on there because he

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:48.699
or she is doing what he or she has to do in
order to make the production happen and they

00:07:48.699 --> 00:07:54.331
may be making a lot of different creative
suggestions and creative changes. But it doesn’t

00:07:54.331 --> 00:07:55.331
make them the writer.

00:07:55.331 --> 00:07:56.850
Film Courage: So again, stay in your lane?

00:07:56.850 --> 00:08:01.300
Shannan: Stay in your lane, that’s it! You’ve
got to know what your lane is and stay in

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:01.370
it.

